[{"id":"VRCE-1-35-1","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":1,"english":"When the ascetic had spoken thus, both the heroes, Rāma and Lakşmaņa, saluting that first of anchorites, said,","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.925,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035001","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f7365ec48d63c29d25b88bff41038173941054b88d41993dcc9c96c8e232da76","compact":"98b72c3047d6ffd8762df27c148e8634748aab3dcfe9737e4b26088ab68b3f15","nasal":"daa2f1317c25c95b6b5cff7f19aadd7bcf5b2d24b25e99b8e2be1cb0a76db2c8"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-2","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":2,"english":"O Brāhmaṇa, you have delivered this noble narration fraught with morality. Now it behoves you to speak about the elder daughter of the mountain-king. You are extensively conversant with everything relative to men or gods.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.891,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035002","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"35300399740157d9bf81f9f6ffac84cff958a521344c963c64c81cbed38703af","compact":"5b594da20b3aa81034811454a640d504d85b79258aa7f085fd10112293b23e98","nasal":"5b594da20b3aa81034811454a640d504d85b79258aa7f085fd10112293b23e98"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-3","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":3,"english":"O Brāhmaṇa, you have delivered this noble narration fraught with morality. Now it behoves you to speak about the elder daughter of the mountain-king. You are extensively conversant with everything relative to men or gods. Why is it that purifier of the worlds lave three directions? And why is that foremost of streams, Gangā, famous as wending in three ways","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.377,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-2","DUTT-1-36-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035003","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ecdd094802db7a26068fb0027be8c5a7971397f656685eb68ebcc7c1bd69a6a6","compact":"c25c5d1a0eae082aa2da8dcaac17c986f2f6a09d56c1bd31c6d92e624bbbc15d","nasal":"c25c5d1a0eae082aa2da8dcaac17c986f2f6a09d56c1bd31c6d92e624bbbc15d"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-4","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":4,"english":"Why is it that purifier of the worlds lave three directions? And why is that foremost of streams, Gangā, famous as wending in three ways And, O you cognizant of morality, what are her performances in the three worlds? Thereat Viśvāmitra having asceticism for this wealth, began to relate to Käkutstha that history in detail in the midst of the ascetics.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.462,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-3","DUTT-1-36-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035004","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"63701008df7f2a7e04ec80e09ed5e4e991092011df2a7154fe205360e74fb6df","compact":"0af86adbcd2c5202d49dd990d8e886a57a7313c291299b884f348b511c1b2f41","nasal":"19fb20d1a2ee42786d22421d3c11ebf33006e77ad668fbda5c6a46e62827cd7e"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-5","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":5,"english":"And, O you cognizant of morality, what are her performances in the three worlds? Thereat Viśvāmitra having asceticism for this wealth, began to relate to Käkutstha that history in detail in the midst of the ascetics. In days of yore, O Rāma, the blue-throated one of mighty asceticism, having entered into matrimony, commenced upon knowing the goddess. And as that intelligent blue-throated god, Mahadeva, was thus engaged in sport, a divine hundred years passed away.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.485,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-4","DUTT-1-36-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035005","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d5121d73edb15144b9e5038d8a1b5eacccf406a6e107ea0ed10f8112e490a2a9","compact":"3cac7568eb301c6c4bafdd1a34f10a59b2ef7ad9182f93855c9bbe63e767288f","nasal":"3cac7568eb301c6c4bafdd1a34f10a59b2ef7ad9182f93855c9bbe63e767288f"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-6","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":6,"english":"In days of yore, O Rāma, the blue-throated one of mighty asceticism, having entered into matrimony, commenced upon knowing the goddess. And as that intelligent blue-throated god, Mahadeva, was thus engaged in sport, a divine hundred years passed away.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.806,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035006","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"359bbdc01beaf5e0a8d040b8d76914c018eec25fd6e46bed5822bd89e19e2f91","compact":"1428997410e6d4993744791a1f267ba27ec7cfb6eb9e541e865c68601c29d4b9","nasal":"4d102d839244f6e7a0f42bb19f67303ce3ece43eeaddc6abaa45f344749a16a8"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-7","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":7,"english":"In days of yore, O Rāma, the blue-throated one of mighty asceticism, having entered into matrimony, commenced upon knowing the goddess. And as that intelligent blue-throated god, Mahadeva, was thus engaged in sport, a divine hundred years passed away. And yet, O Rāma, chastiser of foes, no son was born of her. Thereat all the gods with the Grand-sire at their head became exceedingly anxious.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.509,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-5","DUTT-1-36-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035007","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"39386559b2a6da319801aa06a3ad557ed1f636ad89783c010977ad9888231dad","compact":"46ee52d3080d59a337708b15c00f9fa6c3e5017b721c6ed2f1e20d3992e108ff","nasal":"97b09905edfa232c809e5241893dfe3f6e8cd01ed1362c2af03e93bc10dce493"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-8","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":8,"english":"Who will be able to bear the offspring of this union? And thereupon the celestials repairing to Mahadeva, thus addressed him, saluting low,","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.444,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035008","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cae663112bd61a01e4d036563ea0a2baea908dd5dbf14456f4f86e174f39b9a9","compact":"77af450ff674d95e41235cebc770f545cccea17f6896ea36b35afcb57ffdce19","nasal":"77af450ff674d95e41235cebc770f545cccea17f6896ea36b35afcb57ffdce19"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-9","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":9,"english":"Who will be able to bear the offspring of this union? And thereupon the celestials repairing to Mahadeva, thus addressed him, saluting low, O god of gods! O mighty deity! ever engaged in the welfare of all, it behove you to be propitious at the humble salutations of the celestials.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.609,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-7","DUTT-1-36-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035009","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2aeb652cec69bae2b34941980b5ca82677295eddf201025faf4af189a4e26cfa","compact":"cf69f914724e2ab51bdcb50280e0b3a65d266b4b8496a98307b46eebc573c088","nasal":"ffbf212029b9a9f4818ce826668685ecb209deae25c9e14d32117a4cf64d197f"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-10","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":10,"english":"The worlds, O foremost of celestials, are incapable of bearing your energy. Therefore, for the welfare of the three worlds, do you, being furnished with Brahmā asceticism, in company with the goddess practise austerities, and rein in your energy by your native indomitableness.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.86,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035010","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ff3066b1399f580a6df64917336d8d92c2d4058638108c6ef2fae1cbe521ec91","compact":"02ba0f70d70e58228bda607f2fe481809a41fc208577b059013b9fd7ee7a999f","nasal":"5a80d73aa664d03e568d08b523befa0121a319e8354235cc56ac5b10c91fcff7"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-11","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":11,"english":"Do you preserve these worlds; for it become you not to destroy all.'","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.879,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035011","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"dc2b65ef44ca562cdd0ddcd29757833fefc5ffc58d7b675e46782810241cf892","compact":"fe7766eef0d932bec5e8d23c2728044c45e6124a98a5b17d94e403a121bce367","nasal":"03b99d0eaead1bda198da78c9332262b82f5c79f7d4d5fb024444d6051203fbe"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-12","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":12,"english":"Hearing the words of the deities, the great god of the worlds said to them, 'So be it!' And addressing them again he said,","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035012","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bc84e898fb2a2df1433631122a83afb3e1bb229cc491dc5d3addbf4f1688ba3c","compact":"d466537b3f75d86c3711c75fcb097179b3a7e2f0e4a806ad03629e86184905af","nasal":"d466537b3f75d86c3711c75fcb097179b3a7e2f0e4a806ad03629e86184905af"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-13","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":13,"english":"You gods, by my own energy I will assisted by Uma bear my virile vigour, therefore let the creation find rest!","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.831,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035013","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5e2b2c93183b7e5d1a08216280ef9cf05ee6975d7d10f8a4fc9853f1c3e2855a","compact":"a69f4a77780217383b741da0ff0ff9e038047df78074ed2ec1c2198e70f4c3b9","nasal":"a69f4a77780217383b741da0ff0ff9e038047df78074ed2ec1c2198e70f4c3b9"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-14","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":14,"english":"But tell me, you foremost of celestials, who will sustain my potent virility rushing out from its receptacle?'","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035014","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"553e7b342aba26951a85869aae06165085f97ad6ccbfce9cfe136f31dfe74017","compact":"f3ef17ddfad36a9a4f5b4a269c1929060689a5b598cf381f1930c90fe91cf3c2","nasal":"8cd205f40b9ceb6ec7a18a7d3ee127d3441d396a9eada698bc24a98bbf7210db"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-15","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":15,"english":"Being thus addressed, the gods answered him having the bull for his mark, ‘The earth will today bear your vital flow.'","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.843,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035015","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3ac3cf76b431f2a53c503064159de16acfa8d982ae9c003a18f01b02d3af02cb","compact":"a8081c4dca9f7e1731c665208f21e12f32e848cae581ab5dcd981dd4214382b9","nasal":"a8081c4dca9f7e1731c665208f21e12f32e848cae581ab5dcd981dd4214382b9"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-16","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":16,"english":"Thus assured, the mighty lord of the celestials let go his vital fluid; and thereat the earth containing mountains and forests was overspread with the energy.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.796,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035016","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"18deba4299b4572818bd12fe01984df6f24c899fab5c5b417a976f84494c2b9e","compact":"a07e29c7b8defec8f02d2db56a7a08da108c596a6b6da5183fbc0e1411b31399","nasal":"a07e29c7b8defec8f02d2db56a7a08da108c596a6b6da5183fbc0e1411b31399"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-17","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":17,"english":"Then the gods spoke to the Fire, saying, 'Do you in company with the Wind entrain to this fierce an mighty energy!'","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.695,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035017","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ecc7ebf2b24a1719c31e66d64a78c9a1103b85d03f98cbb6ca9c15d753700555","compact":"308e4c9f9478713ed146c2e15eac483dbf9bfec70b0e18c4aa6387fb8a7e47d8","nasal":"c7d5c8411778951b76d75777d7da97661c5d2323d35336cdbc463269880e23bc"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-18","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":18,"english":"When the Wind had entered into it, it was developed into a white hill, and a forest of glossy reeds, resembling fire or the Sun.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.474,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-17"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035018","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9285e26a747e1446fc71d48e98126008250feedf4f7400255ad291728af23981","compact":"8d32d0e39f1ccff2eaa695dd04dcb5af7f4e49c93e81c564496f2ca9bbc0add0","nasal":"8d32d0e39f1ccff2eaa695dd04dcb5af7f4e49c93e81c564496f2ca9bbc0add0"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-19","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":19,"english":"Here sprang from Fire Kārtikeya of mighty energy. And thereupon the celestials and the saints, with gratified hearts, began to pay enthusiastic adorations to Uma and Siva.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.825,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035019","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f90d6177c8c40e06b9ac64eebbf5b631c0b0bff60fa768b4892142ffabccba58","compact":"d8bca7c63bf247a996e987deb772855311833294164f5a008cf2afd10c324f77","nasal":"d8bca7c63bf247a996e987deb772855311833294164f5a008cf2afd10c324f77"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-20","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":20,"english":"Then the Mountain's daughter, O Rama, addressed the celestials, cursing them with eyes reddened in wrath.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035020","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"57f2352dc332f3dfea88011827359c40ab51f6bbb952863ea48ca0b593069d46","compact":"b07c3903be27c2bc6ecb30a58fae3b0e2664cd0deb15e5dc903224d54ed6fa17","nasal":"b07c3903be27c2bc6ecb30a58fae3b0e2664cd0deb15e5dc903224d54ed6fa17"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-21","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":21,"english":"While in association with Mahadeva for obtaining sons, I was broken in upon by you, for this, you shall not be able yourselves to beget offspring on your wives. And from this day forth, your wives shall remain without issue.'","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.908,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035021","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"77681240ea08a50d20dbc5e292230e117515eaf7177a9f905142b03c3b913a57","compact":"a24662a7c6437a812a0e3567dccb78d450aef72635faaccca6b10dc413e6a61b","nasal":"57437d51b741ddadd2c408c47de522c60018a8950197bdc546a767575f0c5147"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-22","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":22,"english":"Having thus spoke to the celestials, she cursed the Earth also, saying, 'O Earth, you will have multiform surface and many husbands.'","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.852,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035022","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f92b6b5bd086542441e1091bf66bb997b09b1909890a25983556a61567c92b77","compact":"c003521956cb4c395d1342b2075bae2ecb703faa0348d1ce38e88f0b925c8b56","nasal":"c003521956cb4c395d1342b2075bae2ecb703faa0348d1ce38e88f0b925c8b56"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-23","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":23,"english":"Nor, stained because of my ire, shall you experience the pleasure that is felt on obtaining a son, O you of wicked understanding, O you that do not wish me a son!","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.884,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035023","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d27a95ca83f623fa57458f2f3edf41515dc051f93f4f7e63b6858b8fa38a8697","compact":"085ddf29073404ad10aa8d6e45321ef31f9c0f60e1e29b58f633f65fccd8bb5e","nasal":"085ddf29073404ad10aa8d6e45321ef31f9c0f60e1e29b58f633f65fccd8bb5e"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-24","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":24,"english":"Witnessing the gods, thus distressed, the lord of the celestials set out in the direction presided over by Varuna (The West).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.853,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-23"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035024","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c552b412a7c4f5138c55f39086de99bc3af85aa71c0ff795177ddf0e003f22d8","compact":"89bf9175ddab91593ee18e79fa9b94cae240eef4eb7b9c7707c771863d468139","nasal":"178c812299da6defefa9c1a8fe858f6a32f5ba947b88fedd7b70da34a218da91"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-25","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":25,"english":"And having repaired to the north side of that mountain, Mahesvara along with the goddess became engaged in austerities on the peak Himavatprabhava.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.879,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035025","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d2c953ea4d172ab7514a1b388a983ff89b7924af4ae40e786898c8e79929da57","compact":"6212e52714f18416af689c40caffe06b4cbc539c003324a938bd3e15129b3946","nasal":"2df8c64d61f5bed320d3ec72b8bf20bdf78bbaf56afa1de5933a915963ce0b13"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-35-26","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":35,"verse":26,"english":"I have now related to you, O Rāma, the spread of the Mountain's daughter, (Gangā). Do you now together with Lakşmaņa listen to the narration of Bhāgīrathi's potency.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.983,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-36-25"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1035026","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e148ca574aa86c9d851c8bcb9a312bd4fcc9646f8c96506a8e32c9a565dd5a95","compact":"04b3d3b51fbd3b279d62c8a400d626b1b3e1567074bf2bacfa111d1b7392101c","nasal":"154693cc78cff6de0b130e44cf6b8d988e5f9e12cab8ab496dadcc9467a3f458"}}]
